Experiment 2.1: Search Insert Position (Leetcode Problem 35)

Aim:Given a sorted integer array nums and an integer target, return the index if the target is found. If not, return the index where it would be inserted in order.

Algorithm:

1.) Start
2.) Take the input sorted array nums and integer target.
3.) Initialize low = 0 and high = nums.size() - 1.
4.) Perform Binary Search while low <= high:
5.) Calculate the middle index mid.
6.) Compare nums[mid] with target:
• If nums[mid] == target, return mid.
• If nums[mid] > target, search the left half by setting high = mid - 1.
• If nums[mid] < target, search the right half by setting low = mid + 1.
7.) If the target is not found, return low as the correct insertion position.
8.) End

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    int searchInsert(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
        int low=0;
        int high=nums.size()-1;
        int mid=(low+high)/2;

        while(low<=high){

            if(nums[mid]==target){
                return mid;
            }
            if(nums[mid]>target){
                high=mid-1;
                mid=low+(high-low)/2;
            }
            if(nums[mid]<target){
                low=mid+1;
                mid=low+(high-low)/2;
            }
        }
        return low;
    }
};

Experiment 2.2: Search in Rotated Sorted Array (Leetcode Problem 33)

Aim:Given a rotated sorted integer array nums and an integer target, return the index of target if it exists in the array; otherwise, return -1.

Algorithm:

1.) Start
2.) Take the input rotated sorted array nums and integer target.
3.) Initialize low = 0 and high = nums.size() - 1.
4.) Perform Binary Search while low <= high.
5.) Calculate the middle index mid.
6.) If nums[mid] == target, return mid.
7.) Check which half of the array is sorted:
If the left half (nums[low] to nums[mid]) is sorted:
If target lies between nums[low] and nums[mid], search the left half by setting high = mid - 1.
Otherwise, search the right half by setting low = mid + 1.
Otherwise, the right half (nums[mid] to nums[high]) is sorted:
If target lies between nums[mid] and nums[high], search the right half by setting low = mid + 1.
Otherwise, search the left half by setting high = mid - 1.
8.) If the loop ends without finding the target, return -1.
9.) End

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    int search(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
        int low=0;
        int high=nums.size()-1;

        while(low<=high){
            int mid=low+(high-low)/2;

            if(nums[mid]==target){
                return mid;
            }

            if(nums[low]<=nums[mid]){
                if(target>=nums[low] && target<nums[mid]){
                    high=mid-1;
                }
                else{
                    low=mid+1;
                }
            }
            else{
                if(target>nums[mid] && target<=nums[high]){
                    low=mid+1;
                }
                else{
                    high=mid-1;
                }
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
};
